# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: a production TypeError with a null stack was traced to a
# stale-chunk failure. When a client runs a cached index.html whose hashed JS
# chunk a newer deploy has purged, the request for that chunk hits the SPA
# catch-all in public/_redirects (/*  /index.html  200) and comes back as the
# HTML shell with a 200 — NOT a 404. A dynamic import() then receives HTML where
# it expects a JS module and throws (in Safari: a TypeError with no usable
# stack), which the app's top-level error boundary shows as "Something didn't
# load right." A cold client (e.g. a WhatsApp in-app browser opening a shared
# link) with no service worker is the most exposed.
#
# The fix: never let the HTML shell be served stale. index.html (and any SPA
# route that resolves to it) must be revalidated on every load, so the client
# always runs a shell that points at chunks the current deploy still hosts. The
# hashed, content-addressed build assets under /assets/* are immutable by
# construction (their name changes when their content does), so they cache hard.
/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
